item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Review at the sprint close boundary under the authority cited by its manifest. Its item classifications and debt outcomes are results; completing this review does not require every outcome to be satisfied.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 303,
          "quote": "Each classification\ntakes exactly one core value, for the predicate \"the reviewed item is\naccepted as compliant under the sprint authority\"",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line": 300
        }
      ],
      "key": "sprint_close_review",
      "label": "Sprint close review"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The six item outcomes are accepted, local_paydown, design_reframe, requirement_reprice, product_reprice and remove. Debt-entry outcomes are accepted, ticketed, repriced and removed. Each maps to one core value at its close boundary; the repricing layer is a declared change-class fact accompanying the value as domain outcome.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 284,
          "quote": "Each reviewed item is classified as one of `accepted` (compliant under the\nsprint authority), `local_paydown` (local debt remains and is captured by a\npaydown ticket), `design_reframe` (the work needs design repricing),\n`requirement_reprice` (the work needs requirement repricing),\n`product_reprice` (the work changes product definition) or `remove`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line": 279
        }
      ],
      "key": "close_outcome",
      "label": "Close classifications and debt outcomes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A durable unit of work with one authoritative record, stable identity and lifecycle. Its default carrier is one markdown file.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 342,
          "quote": "Each ticket has one durable authoritative record; the default carrier is\none markdown file. A repository or external tracker carrier is lawful only\nwhen it preserves the required ticket contract, history, state transition,\nstable identity, searchability and closure semantics without a co-equal\nlocal copy",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FOne%20durable%20record%20per%20ticket",
          "start_line": 338
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket",
      "label": "Ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One admitted upstream work item with more than one build-tenant execution line, within urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy. The member owns its work-item/ticket-lifecycle relation, while PRODUCT_DEFINITION §8 supplies the context and tenant identities; build-tenant identity and realisation meaning are not redefined here.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 359,
          "quote": "A multi-build-tenant work item is one admitted upstream work item with more\nthan one build-tenant execution line. The upstream authority and each\ntenant-local ticket retain distinct identities and lifecycles.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FMulti-build-tenant%20independence",
          "start_line": 357
        }
      ],
      "key": "multi_build_tenant_work_item",
      "label": "Multi-build-tenant work item"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A ticket carrying one tenant-local execution lifecycle, distinct from upstream authority and sibling tenant tickets. Its build_tenant identifies the applicable product how.build_tenants entry.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 390,
          "quote": "The\n`build_tenant` value identifies the\napplicable entry in the product's `how.build_tenants` definition; a path\nname, display alias or tracker label does not replace that identity unless\nthe product definition binds it as the identity.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FMulti-build-tenant%20independence",
          "start_line": 386
        }
      ],
      "key": "tenant_local_ticket",
      "label": "Tenant-local ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The kind of work record. The minimum common type set is feature, bug, spike and chore, expected to suffice for most cases but extensible by a project.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 422,
          "quote": "The minimum common set of ticket types is `feature`, `bug`, `spike` and\n`chore`. A project may add more; the default set should be enough for\nmost cases",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FTypes",
          "start_line": 420
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket_type",
      "label": "Ticket type"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The execution or migration discipline governing a ticket. Its minimum common set is ordinary and implementation_migration, either available to feature, bug, spike or chore.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 431,
          "quote": "Ticket category is orthogonal to ticket type: type answers what kind of\nwork record this is; category answers what execution or migration\ndiscipline governs the ticket (P3, P1; rc.2 #ticket-category). The minimum\ncommon set is `ordinary` and `implementation_migration`, so a `feature`,\n`bug`, `spike` or `chore` may carry either.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FCategory",
          "start_line": 427
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket_category",
      "label": "Ticket category"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A ticket category for governed replacement of one implementation truth path by another across producers, consumers, projections and proof surfaces. Examples include replacing tracker/read-model authority, migrating every producer and consumer to a new public interface before closure, and removing a bridge or compatibility facade from an authoritative contract family.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 434,
          "quote": "`implementation_migration` is\nused when the ticket is not \"change code until tests pass\" but a governed\nreplacement of one implementation truth path with another across producers,\nconsumers, projections and proof surfaces",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FCategory",
          "start_line": 431
        }
      ],
      "key": "implementation_migration",
      "label": "Implementation migration ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The intake-declared lawful constitutional re-entry class, using SPEC_METHOD §4. Common, non-exhaustive pairings are feature with goal_reprice, intent_reprice, product_reprice, requirement_reprice or design_reframe; bug with requirement_reprice, design_reframe or realization_refactor; chore with design_reframe or realization_refactor. A spike remains exploratory but carries its current triage class.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 464,
          "quote": "Ticket type and change class are orthogonal: type answers what kind of\nwork record this is; change class answers where the change lawfully\nre-enters the constitutional chain. A ticket records the `change_class`\ndeclared by intake triage, using the classes of\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#change-classes)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FChange%20class",
          "start_line": 459
        }
      ],
      "key": "change_class",
      "label": "Change class"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Declared carrier states include ticket statuses, local added states, lanes, sprint statuses, migration_strategy, library_usage and change_class. Minimum ticket statuses are backlog, active and completed. They are not evaluation results and map to no core value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 540,
          "quote": "The ticket statuses, any local state a project adds, the lanes below, the\nsprint statuses of §3 and the `migration_strategy` and `library_usage`\nvalues of §6 are declared states of their carriers, not results, and map\nto no core value; the recorded `change_class` is a declared state under\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#change-classes).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FStatus",
          "start_line": 536
        }
      ],
      "key": "carrier_state",
      "label": "Declared carrier state"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Backlog is accepted durable work outside current execution focus that should remain visible; active is the current bounded execution set; completed is closed work retained as history. Product bindings ticketing.tickets.lanes.backlog, .active and .completed locate these lanes, defaulting respectively to .ai-workspace/tickets/backlog/, active/ and completed/. The separation keeps live search small and readable without relocating deferred work into goals or comments.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 559,
          "quote": "`backlog`\nis accepted durable work that should remain visible but is not part of the\ncurrent execution focus; `active` is the current bounded execution set;\n`completed` is closed work retained as history.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FLanes",
          "start_line": 556
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket_lane",
      "label": "Ticket lanes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Family F13 concerns which carrier holds work, what it may claim and when it closes. Instance premises are differing lifetimes, multiple apparent state surfaces, multiple build tenants, migrations between authoritative truth paths and variable carrier technologies. The table presents attributed consequences and separately owned policy choices; it does not decide beyond cited rules.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 586,
          "quote": "This family (F13 in the index of\n[FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundation-to-law))\ndecides which record carries a piece of work, what that record may claim\nand when the work closes.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line": 574
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_carrier_family",
      "label": "F13 work-carrier family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One run-scoped contract for each execution governed by the represented method, derived from the smallest lawful carrier selected by work/obligation lifetime after establishing upstream authority. Ticket-shaped means execution-contract fields, not a durable ticket identity or lane state. Contract states include drafted, admitted, rejected and superseded.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 634,
          "quote": "Every execution governed by this method uses one run-scoped execution\ncontract. The required lifetime of the work or obligation selects the\nsmallest lawful source carrier: first establish the applicable upstream\nwork authority, then select the first matching row below.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FCarrier%20selection",
          "start_line": 629
        },
        {
          "end_line": 691,
          "quote": "the contract carries at minimum the states named in ACTIVATION §1\n(drafted, admitted, rejected, superseded).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FExecution%20contract%20admission",
          "start_line": 690
        }
      ],
      "key": "execution_contract",
      "label": "Execution contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The local gain function of the work: evaluation_criteria express progress/completion and non_closure_conditions express failures that remain despite locally green proof.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 713,
          "quote": "An execution contract should expose both what counts as progress or\ncompletion and what still counts as failure even when some local proof is\ngreen. At minimum it should make `evaluation_criteria` and\n`non_closure_conditions` visible. These are the local gain function for\nthe work",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FEvaluation%20criteria%20and%20non-closure%20conditions",
          "start_line": 709
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_and_non_closure",
      "label": "Evaluation criteria and non-closure conditions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Ticket and work-instruction terms resolved under PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5. Their relevant bounded-context meaning depends on the cited product, re-entry surface, execution basis and affected scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 736,
          "quote": "Ticket and work-instruction terms resolve under the bounded-context\nsemantic resolution of PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5. A ticket may use an\nunqualified term when its cited product, re-entry surface, execution basis\nand affected scope select exactly one bounded-context meaning",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FBounded-context%20term%20binding",
          "start_line": 732
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_term_binding",
      "label": "Bounded-context work-term binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The explicit declaration and live markdown checklist constituting ticket-local authority for implementation migration. Strategy values are inside_out_hard_break and fundamental_re_adoption; library_usage values are none, consume and extend.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 756,
          "quote": "A ticket with `ticket_category: implementation_migration` carries both an\nexplicit migration declaration block and a hard migration checklist. Both\nare part of the ticket authority, not optional commentary.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FDeclaration%20and%20checklist",
          "start_line": 754
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_declaration_checklist",
      "label": "Migration declaration and checklist"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The review of repeated realisation patterns and the declaration of boundary-local rebuilding, consumption of an existing reusable library/commonisation surface, or extension of that surface before local implementation lands.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 864,
          "quote": "An implementation migration ticket declares whether the active boundary is\nrebuilding a pattern locally, consuming an existing reusable library or\ncommonisation surface, or extending that surface before the local\nimplementation lands; `library_usage` records which",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FRecurring%20realisation%20and%20library%20declaration",
          "start_line": 860
        }
      ],
      "key": "recurrence_review",
      "label": "Recurrence and library review"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A named walk of every live interface able to bypass, reinterpret, replay or project migrating truth, with an interface-specific closure condition for every item.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 948,
          "quote": "For a core-interface, carrier or authoritative-contract migration, the\nticket includes an `Impacted Interface Review Checklist` before tests can be\nused as closure evidence. The checklist is a named walk of every live\ninterface that can still bypass, reinterpret, replay or project the\nmigrating truth path",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FImpacted%20interface%20review%20checklist",
          "start_line": 944
        }
      ],
      "key": "impacted_interface_checklist",
      "label": "Impacted Interface Review Checklist"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An assessment distinct from construction and owner acceptance. The reviewer is not the reviewed subject's author; a constructor's self-review remains construction evidence. Generic assessment, acceptance and closed-result reuse are attributed respectively to EVALUATION §3, EVALUATION §5 and ACTIVATION §7.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1044,
          "quote": "The generic law is tier 1: a constructor's self-review is construction\noutput and the independent-assessment protocol is\n[EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment); acceptance as the\ndecision owner's disposition, and closure by stated condition rather than\nby reviewer approval, is [EVALUATION §5](EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance); reuse\nof a closed result is [ACTIVATION §7](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state",
          "start_line": 1038
        }
      ],
      "key": "independent_assessment",
      "label": "Independent assessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A residual judgment needed by another activation, recorded once with its owning work and distinguished from a direct or lawfully proxied owner ruling. C, J and O are referenced SPEC_METHOD annotations; J is not O.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1117,
          "quote": "A residual judgment that another activation needs is recorded once, with\nits owning work, in the existing carrier selected under §5.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FRecorded%20judgment%20and%20owner-ruling%20continuity",
          "start_line": 1109
        }
      ],
      "key": "recorded_judgment_and_ruling",
      "label": "Recorded judgment and owner ruling"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An obligation's owner-set post-repricing standing: retained, superseded, deferred or withdrawn. These are recorded states, not assessed-candidate acceptance results or qualified predecessor-claim dispositions, and map to no core value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1161,
          "quote": "The four repricing dispositions are recorded states of the obligation,\nset by its owning authority; they are not results and map to no core\nvalue.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FRecorded%20judgment%20and%20owner-ruling%20continuity",
          "start_line": 1159
        }
      ],
      "key": "obligation_repricing_state",
      "label": "Obligation repricing disposition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A bounded claim whose completion is governed by evidence for every applicable obligation, applicable required judgments and owner rulings, absence of active non-closure conditions and any reserved owner acceptance. This member owns the work-claim closure condition; SPEC_METHOD §4 applies it to a change span.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1186,
          "quote": "A work claim is complete only when every applicable declared obligation is\nsatisfied by valid evidence for its exact subject, basis and scope; every\nrequired judgment and owner ruling is present and applicable; and no active\nnon-closure condition remains.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FCondition-based%20closure%20and%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1180
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_claim",
      "label": "Work claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A proof projection from admitted work authority. A substantive test should derive through ticket, intake triage, lawful re-entry, governing requirement/design/module/published function/carrier/closure surface and then expectation. Implementation behaviour is evidence, not the authority for expected results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1222,
          "quote": "ticket -> intake triage -> lawful re-entry point -> governing requirement,\ndesign, module, published function, carrier, or closure surface -> test expectation",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FTest%20case%20authority",
          "start_line": 1221
        }
      ],
      "key": "test_case",
      "label": "Test case"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One work-state authority chain: goals own the bounded work wave, tickets their state, milestone authority subordinate milestones and accepted design realisation decisions. Comments, dashboards, registers, indexes and generated summaries are projections.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1267,
          "quote": "Work state has one authority chain: goals own the bounded work wave, the\nticket owns ticket state, milestone authority owns subordinate milestone\nstate (§1), and accepted design owns realisation decisions. Comments,\ndashboards, registers, indexes and generated summaries are projections",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FOne-truth%20state%20projection%20and%20drift%20refusal%20%28UP-009%29",
          "start_line": 1263
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_authority_chain",
      "label": "Work authority chain and projections"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Three recommended document shapes: sprint manifest, ordinary ticket and migration ticket. They illustrate required fields and sections without instantiating the illustrated work. The sprint example carries id/status/goal/timestamps, Authority, Scope, Excluded Boundaries, Expected Change Classes, Iterations and Closure. The ordinary example carries ticket fields, Context, Acceptance and Links. The migration example carries ticket/migration fields, Migration Declaration and the live Migration Checklist.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1279,
          "quote": "These shapes are the recommended carriers of the fields and sections the\nrules above require; a project may bind another shape that carries the\nsame fields",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F8.%20Recommended%20shapes",
          "start_line": 1277
        }
      ],
      "key": "recommended_carrier_shapes",
      "label": "Recommended carrier templates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The default local-file carrier is used."],
      "definition": "Humans and agents can read the active folder directly; backlog remains searchable durable work outside immediate focus; rg suffices for locating active work, without a required database or board. Illustrative commands search headings/status/type/goal in active and backlog folders, and T-001/B-002 identities across tickets.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1428,
          "quote": "The default local-file carrier assumes that humans and agents can read the\nactive ticket folder directly; that backlog remains searchable durable work\nwithout competing with the active lane for immediate attention; that `rg`\nis sufficient for finding active work; and that no database or board is\nrequired",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F9.%20Operating%20mode%20and%20relationships%2FOperating%20mode",
          "start_line": 1424
        }
      ],
